{"meta":{"chapter":"02","section":"03","title":"Impulse and Momentum","course":"dynamics","learning_objectives":[{"id":"dynamics-02-03-lo-01","statement":"Define linear momentum and impulse"},{"id":"dynamics-02-03-lo-02","statement":"Apply the impulse-momentum theorem"},{"id":"dynamics-02-03-lo-03","statement":"Use conservation of momentum for collisions"},{"id":"dynamics-02-03-lo-04","statement":"Distinguish elastic and inelastic collisions"}],"concept_ids":["linear_momentum","impulse_momentum","conservation_of_momentum","collision"],"physics_prereqs":["newtons_second_law"],"math_prereqs":["integrals"],"units_focus":["kg·m/s","N·s"],"common_errors":["sign","vector","average_vs_impulse"],"estimated_time_minutes":55,"prereq_section_ids":["dynamics/02/01"],"practice_ids":["momentum_numeric_basic","impulse_numeric_basic","collision_stick_numeric","impulse_momentum_steps","collision_type_concept","system_boundary_concept"],"lo_practice_map":{"dynamics-02-03-lo-01":["impulse_momentum_steps","momentum_numeric_basic","system_boundary_concept"],"dynamics-02-03-lo-02":["impulse_numeric_basic"],"dynamics-02-03-lo-03":["collision_stick_numeric"],"dynamics-02-03-lo-04":["collision_type_concept"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Impulse and Momentum\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Concept relationship map connecting Linear Momentum, Impulse Momentum, and Conservation Of Momentum within Impulse and Momentum.\" conceptid=\"linear_momentum\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/reference-figures/dynamics/02-03-impulse-and-momentum.png\" alt=\"Impulse and Momentum concept map with Linear Momentum, Impulse Momentum, and Conservation Of Momentum connected to the section focus.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n\n> **\"When forces act for a very short time, momentum often tells the story more cleanly than acceleration.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n`newtons_second_law`\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\n1. **Define** linear momentum and impulse, and state their units and vector nature\n2. **Apply** the impulse-momentum theorem over a finite time interval, including the average-force approximation\n3. **Use** conservation of momentum for isolated systems, with correct sign conventions\n4. **Distinguish** elastic, inelastic, and perfectly inelastic collisions, and apply the coefficient of restitution\n\n---\n\n## Opening: The Egg, the Crumple Zone, and the Airbag\n\nDrop an egg onto a concrete floor and it shatters. Drop the identical egg from the identical height onto a thick foam pad and it survives. The egg arrives with exactly the same momentum in both cases, and in both cases it ends at rest, so the *change in momentum* is identical. What differs is the time over which that change happens. Concrete stops the egg in roughly a millisecond; foam stretches the same stop over tens of milliseconds. Because impulse equals force multiplied by time, lengthening the stopping time slashes the peak force.\n\nThis single idea is the reason cars have crumple zones, helmets have liners, and gymnasts land on mats. Mercedes-Benz's 1952 crumple-zone patent (the idea reached production in 1959) did not make collisions gentler in any energy sense. It made them *slower*, deliberately sacrificing the front of the car so the passenger cabin decelerates over a longer time and therefore at a lower force.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** When loading is too brief or too violent to track instant by instant, do not chase acceleration. Track the accumulated effect of force over time. That accumulated effect is impulse, and it changes momentum.\n\nForce-based analysis works well when the force history is simple. Impacts, collisions, and short bursts of loading are usually too brief to model point by point. In those cases what matters is the total effect of force over time, which is exactly what momentum methods capture:\n\n- a bat striking a ball\n- a crash absorber reducing peak force by lengthening impact time\n- a docking cart coupling to another cart\n- a robotic gripper delivering a short impulse to reposition a part\n\n---\n\n## Linear Momentum\n\nLinear momentum is the product of mass and velocity:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\vec{p} = m\\vec{v}}\n$$\n\n| Property | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Units | kg*m/s or N*s |\n| Vector? | Yes |\n| Depends on direction? | Yes |\n\nMomentum is not the same as kinetic energy. A heavy slow object and a light fast object may have comparable momentum but very different kinetic energies, because momentum scales with $v$ while kinetic energy scales with $v^2$. This $v$ versus $v^2$ distinction is the deepest reason momentum and energy give different answers about collisions.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Object A is 8 kg at 2 m/s; object B is 2 kg at 8 m/s. Compare their momentum and kinetic energy.\" explanation=\"Momentum: p_A = 8*2 = 16 kg*m/s and p_B = 2*8 = 16 kg*m/s, so they are equal. Kinetic energy: T_A = 0.5*8*2^2 = 16 J but T_B = 0.5*2*8^2 = 64 J, four times larger. Identical momentum, very different energy, because energy scales with v^2.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Equal momentum, but B has four times the kinetic energy\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Equal momentum and equal kinetic energy\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A has more momentum because it is heavier\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## Impulse\n\nImpulse is the time-accumulated effect of force:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\vec{J} = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\vec{F}\\,dt}\n$$\n\nIf the force can be approximated by an average value over the interval,\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{avg}\\,\\Delta t}\n$$\n\nGraphically, impulse is the area under the force-time curve. A tall, narrow spike (hard contact) and a short, wide hump (soft contact) can enclose the same area, and therefore deliver the same impulse, while producing wildly different peak forces.\n\n---\n\n## Impulse-Momentum Theorem\n\n### Derivation from Newton's Second Law\n\nThe theorem is not a separate axiom. It is Newton's second law integrated over time. Starting from $\\vec{F} = m\\,d\\vec{v}/dt$ for constant mass,\n\n$$\n\\vec{F}\\,dt = m\\,d\\vec{v}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides from $t_1$ to $t_2$,\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\vec{F}\\,dt = m\\int_{v_1}^{v_2} d\\vec{v} = m\\vec{v}_2 - m\\vec{v}_1\n$$\n\nThe left side is the impulse and the right side is the change in momentum, giving\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\vec{J} = \\Delta \\vec{p} = m\\vec{v}_2 - m\\vec{v}_1}\n$$\n\nThis theorem is especially useful when the force varies strongly with time, the contact duration is short, or only the initial and final velocities matter. It lets us bypass the messy, unknown force history and work only with endpoints.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Average Impact Force from a Momentum Change: F_avg = m*(v2 - v1)/dt\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"m\",\"label\":\"Body mass\",\"min\":0.1,\"max\":1500,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"kg\",\"default\":0.145},{\"name\":\"v1\",\"label\":\"Incoming speed (toward wall)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":60,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"default\":40},{\"name\":\"v2\",\"label\":\"Outgoing speed (rebound)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":60,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"default\":50},{\"name\":\"dt\",\"label\":\"Contact time\",\"min\":0.0005,\"max\":0.3,\"step\":0.0005,\"unit\":\"s\",\"default\":0.0007}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Impulse delivered\",\"unit\":\"N*s\",\"expr\":\"m*(v2 + v1)\"},{\"label\":\"Average force\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"m*(v2 + v1)/dt\"},{\"label\":\"Average force in g-units\",\"unit\":\"x mg\",\"expr\":\"(v2 + v1)/(dt*9.81)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"dt\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nDrag the contact-time slider and watch the average force plunge as \\$dt\\$ grows: this single inverse relationship is why crumple zones, airbags, and crash cushions all chase a longer stopping time for the same momentum change.\n\n<dynamic-scenario\n  topic=\"Crash Cushion Design\"\n  context=\"A transportation engineer is comparing two highway crash cushions that stop the same maintenance cart. One stops the cart in 0.08 s, and the other in 0.20 s. The design team wants to reduce the average impact force without changing the vehicle mass or entry speed.\"\n  course=\"dynamics\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## System Thinking in Momentum Problems\n\nBefore writing equations, identify the system boundary.\n\n### Single-particle system\n\nUse impulse-momentum directly for one body when the external force history is known.\n\n### Multi-particle system\n\nUse conservation of momentum when the net external impulse is negligible compared with the internal collision forces.\n\nThis is the part many students miss: momentum is conserved for a **system**, not automatically for every individual body. During a collision the internal forces are enormous (the carts shove each other hard) but they are equal and opposite, so they cancel within the system. External forces such as gravity and friction are still present, but over a millisecond-scale impact they deliver negligible impulse compared with the collision itself.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Reducing Peak Force on a Highway Crash Cushion\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A maintenance cart hits your cushion at a fixed mass and entry speed. The required momentum change is therefore fixed. To cut the average force on the cart, what is the most direct lever?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Stretch the stopping time by adding crushable depth.\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Right. With m and delta-v fixed, F_avg = delta-p / dt falls as dt grows. Adding stroke length stretches dt.\"},{\"label\":\"Make the cushion stiffer so it stops the cart faster.\",\"next\":\"lose\",\"feedback\":\"Stiffer means a shorter dt for the same momentum change, which raises the average force. That is the wrong direction.\"},{\"label\":\"Increase the cart mass with ballast.\",\"next\":\"lose\",\"feedback\":\"You cannot change the incoming cart, and more mass would only increase the momentum change you must absorb.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"You double the crush stroke, roughly doubling the stopping time from 0.08 s to 0.16 s. What happens to the average force?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It is roughly halved, since F_avg is inversely proportional to dt.\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. The same impulse spread over twice the time gives about half the average force.\"},{\"label\":\"It stays the same because the impulse is unchanged.\",\"next\":\"lose\",\"feedback\":\"The impulse (area under F-t) is indeed unchanged, but spreading it over more time lowers the average and peak force.\"}]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"Marketing asks whether the longer cushion also absorbs more energy. What is the honest answer?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No: the kinetic energy removed is fixed by the entry speed; the cushion only changes how the force is delivered over time.\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Momentum and energy answer different questions; the cushion reshapes the force-time profile, not the total energy that must be dissipated.\"},{\"label\":\"Yes: a longer cushion removes more kinetic energy.\",\"next\":\"lose\",\"feedback\":\"Energy removed equals the cart KE at impact regardless of stroke. Longer stroke lowers force, not the energy budget.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"You converged on the impulse-momentum design rule: for a fixed momentum change, the only way to cut peak force is to lengthen contact time, and that is independent of the energy that must still be dissipated.\",\"choices\":[]},\"lose\":{\"prompt\":\"This path either shortens the stopping time or confuses the momentum question with the energy question. Recall F_avg = delta-p / dt with delta-p fixed, then try again.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Conservation of Momentum\n\nIf the external net impulse on the system is zero or negligible,\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\vec{p}_{total,1} = \\vec{p}_{total,2}}\n$$\n\nFor two particles in one dimension:\n\n$$\nm_A v_{A1} + m_B v_{B1} = m_A v_{A2} + m_B v_{B2}\n$$\n\nDirection must be handled with signs. A leftward velocity is not \"just slower.\" It is a negative velocity under a right-positive sign convention.\n\n---\n\n## Collision Types and the Coefficient of Restitution\n\n| Type | Momentum | Kinetic energy |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Elastic | Conserved | Conserved |\n| Inelastic | Conserved | Not conserved |\n| Perfectly inelastic | Conserved | Not conserved, bodies stick |\n\nThe easiest mistake here is to remember only that \"momentum is conserved\" and forget that kinetic energy is **not** generally conserved except in elastic collisions.\n\nThe bridge between these categories is the **coefficient of restitution** $e$, defined as the ratio of relative separation speed to relative approach speed along the line of impact:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{e = \\frac{v_{B2} - v_{A2}}{v_{A1} - v_{B1}}}\n$$\n\nHere $e = 1$ is perfectly elastic (no kinetic energy lost), $e = 0$ is perfectly inelastic (the bodies move together afterward), and real impacts fall in between (steel on steel is roughly 0.6, a hardwood floor and a basketball roughly 0.75). Pairing this single equation with momentum conservation gives two equations for the two unknown final velocities of any one-dimensional impact.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 2.3: Two Carts Stick Together (Perfectly Inelastic)\n\n**Problem:** Cart A (2 kg) moves at 5 m/s to the right. Cart B (3 kg) is at rest. They collide and stick together. Find the final velocity.\n\n**Step A: Define the system.** Choose the two-cart system together. During the short collision we neglect external impulse compared with the large internal collision forces. Take rightward as positive.\n\n**Step B: Write momentum conservation.** Because the carts stick, they share one final velocity $v_f$:\n\n$$\nm_A v_{A1} + m_B v_{B1} = (m_A + m_B)v_f\n$$\n\n**Step C: Substitute values.**\n\n$$\n(2)(5) + (3)(0) = (2+3)v_f\n$$\n\n$$\n10 = 5 v_f \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad v_f = \\boxed{2 \\text{ m/s (rightward)}}\n$$\n\n**Step D: Interpret.** The final speed is smaller than the original 5 m/s because the moving cart shares its momentum with the previously stationary cart. Check the energy: before, $KE_1 = \\tfrac12(2)(5^2) = 25$ J; after, $KE_2 = \\tfrac12(5)(2^2) = 10$ J. So 15 J (60 percent) was lost to deformation and heat, exactly as expected for a perfectly inelastic collision ($e = 0$).\n\n---\n\n### Example 2.4: Average Force in a Short Impact (Impulse-Momentum)\n\n**Problem:** A 0.145 kg baseball arrives at a bat at 40 m/s and leaves at 50 m/s in the opposite direction. The bat and ball are in contact for 0.7 ms (0.0007 s). Find the impulse on the ball and the average force the bat exerts.\n\n**Step 1: Set a sign convention.** Take the outgoing direction (away from the bat, toward the pitcher) as positive. Then the incoming velocity is $v_1 = -40$ m/s and the outgoing velocity is $v_2 = +50$ m/s.\n\n**Step 2: Compute the impulse from the momentum change.**\n\n$$\nJ = m v_2 - m v_1 = (0.145)(50) - (0.145)(-40)\n$$\n\n$$\nJ = 7.25 - (-5.80) = \\boxed{13.05 \\text{ N*s}}\n$$\n\nThe impulse is positive, meaning it points away from the bat, which makes physical sense.\n\n**Step 3: Recover the average force.** Using $J = F_{avg}\\,\\Delta t$,\n\n$$\nF_{avg} = \\frac{J}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{13.05}{0.0007} = \\boxed{18{,}643 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\nThat is about 1.9 metric tons of average force, and the peak force is even higher. This is why a clean hit can shatter a wooden bat: the brief contact time forces an enormous average force to deliver the needed momentum change.\n\n---\n\n### Example 2.5: Elastic Collision with Restitution\n\n**Problem:** A 1 kg cart (A) moving at 4 m/s strikes a stationary 1 kg cart (B) head-on. The collision is elastic ($e = 1$). Find both final velocities.\n\n**Step 1: Momentum conservation (rightward positive).**\n\n$$\n(1)(4) + (1)(0) = (1)v_{A2} + (1)v_{B2} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad v_{A2} + v_{B2} = 4\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Restitution equation with $e = 1$.**\n\n$$\ne = \\frac{v_{B2} - v_{A2}}{v_{A1} - v_{B1}} = \\frac{v_{B2} - v_{A2}}{4 - 0} = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad v_{B2} - v_{A2} = 4\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Solve the two equations.** Adding them: $2 v_{B2} = 8$, so $v_{B2} = \\boxed{4 \\text{ m/s}}$. Then $v_{A2} = 4 - 4 = \\boxed{0 \\text{ m/s}}$.\n\n**Step 4: Interpret.** For equal masses in an elastic collision the carts exchange velocities exactly: A stops and B leaves at A's original speed. Check energy: before, $\\tfrac12(1)(4^2) = 8$ J; after, $\\tfrac12(1)(0^2) + \\tfrac12(1)(4^2) = 8$ J. Kinetic energy is conserved, confirming the collision is elastic. This velocity-exchange result is the principle behind a Newton's cradle.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Solve a Head-On Collision Using Both Governing Equations\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Cart A (1 kg, 4 m/s) hits stationary cart B (1 kg) elastically. Which two equations close the problem?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Momentum conservation and the restitution equation with e = 1\",\"Energy conservation and Newtons second law\",\"Momentum conservation and f_k = mu_k*N\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Any one-dimensional impact is closed by momentum conservation plus the restitution equation. For an elastic hit e = 1; for a sticking hit e = 0.\"},{\"prompt\":\"What does momentum conservation give for the two final velocities?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"v_A2 + v_B2 = 4\",\"v_A2 + v_B2 = 0\",\"v_A2 - v_B2 = 4\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"With equal masses: (1)(4) + (1)(0) = (1)v_A2 + (1)v_B2, so v_A2 + v_B2 = 4 m/s.\"},{\"prompt\":\"What does the restitution equation with e = 1 give?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"v_B2 - v_A2 = 4\",\"v_B2 - v_A2 = 0\",\"v_A2 - v_B2 = 4\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"e = (v_B2 - v_A2)/(v_A1 - v_B1) = (v_B2 - v_A2)/4 = 1, so v_B2 - v_A2 = 4 m/s.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Solving the pair: adding gives 2*v_B2 = 8, so v_B2 = 4 m/s and v_A2 = 0. Equal masses in an elastic collision swap velocities exactly, the Newtons-cradle result.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n## A Fast Diagnostic for Collision Problems\n\nAsk these three questions:\n\n1. What bodies belong in the system?\n2. Is external impulse negligible during the event?\n3. Is kinetic energy also conserved, or only momentum?\n\nThose three questions separate most correct setups from most incorrect ones. If the answer to question 3 is \"energy too,\" use the restitution equation with $e = 1$ as a second equation; if the bodies stick, use $e = 0$ and a single shared final velocity.\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | Role of Impulse and Momentum |\n| --- | --- |\n| Automotive crumple zones | Lengthen impact time to cut peak occupant force for a fixed momentum change |\n| Airbags and helmet liners | Spread the same impulse over more time and area, lowering force and pressure |\n| Rocket and jet propulsion | Thrust is the rate of momentum carried away by exhaust mass |\n| Pile drivers and forging hammers | Deliver a large momentum change in a short impact for high peak force |\n| Spacecraft docking | Low closing speed keeps the coupling impulse and structural loads small |\n| Recoil in firearms and artillery | Conservation of momentum sets recoil velocity from projectile momentum |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** A crash cushion and an airbag use the same equation as a baseball bat. The bat maximizes force by shrinking contact time; the cushion minimizes force by stretching it. Identical physics, opposite design goals.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Linear momentum | $\\vec{p} = m\\vec{v}$ |\n| Impulse (general) | $\\vec{J} = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\vec{F}\\,dt$ |\n| Impulse (average force) | $\\vec{J} = \\vec{F}_{avg}\\,\\Delta t$ |\n| Impulse-momentum theorem | $\\vec{J} = m\\vec{v}_2 - m\\vec{v}_1$ |\n| Conservation of momentum | $\\vec{p}_{total,1} = \\vec{p}_{total,2}$ |\n| Coefficient of restitution | $e = (v_{B2} - v_{A2})/(v_{A1} - v_{B1})$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Momentum tracks the dynamic state of a body through mass and velocity, and is a vector.\n- Impulse is the accumulated effect of force over time and equals the change in momentum.\n- For a fixed momentum change, longer contact time means smaller average force.\n- Conservation of momentum is a *system* statement and depends on negligible external impulse.\n- Kinetic energy is conserved only in elastic collisions; the coefficient of restitution quantifies how much survives.\n- Classify the collision type before solving, and always fix a sign convention first.\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Two skaters push off from rest on frictionless ice. Why must their momenta be equal in magnitude and opposite in direction?\"\n  conceptId=\"momentum\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because they have equal masses.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because the system starts with zero total momentum and the push is an internal interaction.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because momentum is always zero after a collision.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. The skater pair begins with zero total momentum, and the push between them is internal to the system, so the total momentum must remain zero.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"In a perfectly inelastic collision, which quantity is always conserved for an isolated system?\"\n  conceptId=\"collision\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Momentum only\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Kinetic energy only\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Both momentum and kinetic energy\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Neither\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Exactly. Momentum is conserved in isolated collisions, but kinetic energy decreases in a perfectly inelastic collision because some mechanical energy is transformed into deformation, heat, or sound.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"linear_momentum\",\"impulse_momentum\",\"conservation_of_momentum\",\"collision\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn **Chapter 3**, we extend these ideas from particles to rigid bodies that can both translate and rotate, where impulse pairs with angular impulse and momentum pairs with angular momentum.\n\n## References\n\n- OpenStax. (2016). *University Physics, Volume 1.* Sections on Newton’s laws, work and energy, momentum, and rotational dynamics. https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n- OpenStax. (2016). *University Physics, Volume 1.* Chapter 12: Static Equilibrium and Elasticity. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-introduction\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"momentum_numeric_basic","type":"numeric","stem":"A 4 kg ball moves at 8 m/s. Find its momentum.","final_answer":{"value":32,"unit":"kg*m/s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["linear_momentum"],"learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-02-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"impulse_numeric_basic","type":"numeric","stem":"A 60 N average force acts for 0.5 s. Find the impulse.","final_answer":{"value":30,"unit":"N*s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["impulse_momentum"],"learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-02-03-lo-02"]},{"id":"collision_stick_numeric","type":"numeric","stem":"A 5 kg object moving at 10 m/s sticks to a 5 kg stationary object. Find the final velocity.","final_answer":{"value":5,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["conservation_of_momentum","collision"],"learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-02-03-lo-03"]},{"id":"impulse_momentum_steps","type":"step_based","stem":"A 2 kg puck moving right at 3 m/s experiences a net impulse of 10 N*s to the right. Find the final velocity.","steps":[{"step_id":1,"description":"Compute the initial momentum m v_1.","expected_value":6,"unit":"kg*m/s","tolerance":0.1},{"step_id":2,"description":"Add the impulse to find final momentum.","expected_value":16,"unit":"kg*m/s","tolerance":0.1},{"step_id":3,"description":"Divide by mass to find the final velocity.","expected_value":8,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1}],"final_answer":{"value":8,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["impulse_momentum","linear_momentum"],"learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-02-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"collision_type_concept","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"In which collision type do the two bodies definitely leave with the same final velocity?","options":["Elastic","Inelastic","Perfectly inelastic","Glancing"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"If the objects stick together, the collision is perfectly inelastic and both bodies share one final velocity after impact.","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["collision"],"misconception_id":"ke_always_conserved","learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-02-03-lo-04"]},{"id":"system_boundary_concept","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"Why is conservation of momentum easiest to use when external impulse is negligible?","options":["Because mass becomes zero.","Because the total system momentum then changes only through internal interactions that cancel within the system.","Because kinetic energy is automatically conserved too.","Because impulse applies only to isolated systems."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Correct. Internal collision forces come in equal and opposite pairs, so when external impulse is negligible the system's total momentum remains constant.","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["conservation_of_momentum"],"misconception_id":"external_impulse_ignored","learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-02-03-lo-01"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"momentum_scalar_only","pattern":"vector_error","description":"Treating momentum as a magnitude-only quantity","trigger":"learner ignores sign or direction in a one-dimensional collision","feedback":"Momentum is a vector. In one-dimensional problems, the sign convention carries that direction information. Leftward and rightward velocities cannot be added as if they were all positive.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ke_always_conserved","pattern":"collision_model_error","description":"Assuming kinetic energy is conserved in every collision","trigger":"learner uses both momentum and kinetic-energy conservation in an inelastic collision","feedback":"Momentum is conserved in isolated collisions, but kinetic energy is conserved only in elastic collisions. In a perfectly inelastic collision, the bodies stick and kinetic energy decreases.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"external_impulse_ignored","pattern":"system_boundary_error","description":"Using momentum conservation without checking the external impulse assumption","trigger":"learner applies conservation of momentum to a system with significant external forces over the interval","feedback":"Conservation of momentum is a system statement that requires negligible external impulse. Start by defining the system and checking whether the collision interval is short enough for external effects to be ignored.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"impulse_equals_force_only","pattern":"time_integration_error","description":"Confusing impulse with force alone","trigger":"learner omits the time interval or the area-under-curve idea","feedback":"Impulse measures the accumulated effect of force over time. Use J = integral(F dt), or J = F_avg delta t when an average force is appropriate.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"b356c11646677ac6766fc46859fe83c72fad4e0a8f61c344317fcf20cd22846e","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}